  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 142 AM CDT Fri Aug 14 2026\n\nLatest satellite imagery shows shortwave energy moving into\neastern MT and western ND through a broad southwest upper level\nflow. Moisture is streaming into the area from the central \nPacific, combined with strong low level (925-850 MB) moisture \ntransport into northwest ND from the southeast. Strong 850mb FG\nforcing from northeast MT eastward into southern Manitoba is\nproducing rain showers along the northern tier counties as far\neast as the Turtle Mountains. With the approaching shortwave,\nthere has been cooling cloud tops over northeast MT into\nnorthwest MT, with thunder recently reported at Crosby.\n\nFor today, not much has changed as far as the overall forecast.\nnorthwest North Dakota is still the focus for heavy \nprecipitation today. The NAEFS SA page is still pinpointing the \nnorthwest with unusually high PWATs, but high PWATs also extend\na little farther south than 24 hours ago, and even extend into \nsouth central ND by around 00Z Saturday. The ECMWF SA Page \nindicates increased EFI values over far northwest ND. Although \nthe max has shifted a little farther north, high values of 80 to\n90 percent have expanded east, and a little farther south (just\nsouth of Lake Sakakawea) and the shift of tails of 2 has also \nshifted farther east into north central ND. Looking at the HREF\nand REFS, both are indicating the same general area (northwest \nND) for highest QPF today, with the HREF increasing max qpf \nwhile the SPC REFS backs off a bit. Overall though, the areal \nextents are very similar. This increases confidence in the \nnorthwest being the bullseye for heavy qpf, with the areal \nextent of significant precipitation (over an inch) extending \nfrom the west central into the north central (McKenzie county \ninto western Bottineau county) which wasn't much of a change \nfrom 24 hours ago. \n\nThere are some indications of more of an eastward extent in the\nQPF, from southwest into south central ND. However confidence \nis not as great here. The NBM does show that the 50 percent \nprobability of a quarter inch of QPF (through 06Z SAturday) has\nexpanded farther east across southern ND (From Bowman county 24\nhours ago, to the Missouri River from the latest run this \nmorning). The moisture has certainly increased here, however the\nforcing remain strongest from western into north central ND. \nPerhaps qpf from southwest into south central ND today may be \nmore hit and miss than points farther west and north.\n\nOverall, messaging has not changed. The confidence in a Flood \nWatch remains pretty low given the uncertainty for very high \nprecipitation rates with convection. There may still be some \nissues with localized flooding in the northwest, especially for \npoor drainage areas and those areas normally prone to flooding. \n\nWe did lower forecast high temperatures over a good portion of \nwestern ND given the expectation of the cloudy and rainy \nconditions. \n\nAfter precipitation ends late tonight or early Saturday over\ncentral ND, the remainder of the weekend looks to be dry until\nperhaps Sunday evening. But for the most part we expect mainly\ndry conditions with seasonably cool high temperatures in the \n70s through the weekend.\n\nWe may see a brief warmup on Monday with some 80 degree readings\npossibly returning, before a northwest flow takes over and we \nsee temperatures mainly in the 70s with hit and miss chances for\nshowers and thunderstorms through the rest of the workweek.",
